.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAM-LIST-GRANTS 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.73" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eam-list-grants \- list grants: by principal, by role, or a whole account
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eam list-grants
[\fB\-\-principal\fR|\fB\-p\fR \fIERN\fR]
[\fB\-\-role\fR|\fB\-o\fR \fINAME\fR]
[\fB\-\-account\fR|\fB\-a\fR \fIID\fR]
[\fB\-\-page\-size\fR|\fB\-s\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-page\-index\fR|\fB\-i\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-sort\-column\fR|\fB\-c\fR \fICOLUMN\fR]
[\fB\-\-sort\-direction\fR|\fB\-d\fR \fIDIRECTION\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Lists grants, and with them the
.I grantId
that
.BR euclid-cli-eam-revoke-role (1)
takes.
.SH THE THREE QUESTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-principal
"What may they do." Every grant held by one user or one user group.
.TP
.B \-\-role
"Who can do this." Every grant of one role in the account \- which is
what to check before
.BR euclid-cli-eam-update-role (1)
narrows a role or
.BR euclid-cli-eam-delete-role (1)
removes it.
.TP
.B neither
"What is granted here at all." Every grant in the account. This is what
an administration overview wants: a list of users and what each may do is
otherwise one request per user.
.PP
Giving both
.B \-\-principal
and
.B \-\-role
is refused rather than intersected. They are different questions, and the
answer to both at once is one grant or none \- not something worth a
command.
.SH WHAT \-\-PRINCIPAL DOES NOT INCLUDE
That principal's
.I own
grants, and not those of the groups it belongs to.
.PP
So a user who can publish only because they are in a group that was
granted
.I publisher
shows nothing here. That is a different question, and the one
.BR euclid-cli-eam-check-permission (1)
answers \- it counts every group the user is in, the way a real request
does. Reading a user's rights off this command alone will understate
them.
.PP
.B \-\-principal
also reaches across accounts: it answers every grant that principal
holds, in whichever account it was granted, so
.B \-\-account
does not narrow it. The other two forms are account-scoped.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-principal " " \fIERN\fR
A user ERN or a user-group ERN.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-role " " \fINAME\fR
A role name. A built-in name also works.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-account " " \fIID\fR
Account to look in. Your own unless given. Not applied together with
.BR \-\-principal .
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-page\-size " " \fIN\fR
How many grants to return. \fI0\fR, the default, returns all of them.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-page\-index " " \fIN\fR
Zero-based page, applied when \fB\-\-page\-size\fR is set. Defaults to
\fI0\fR.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-sort\-column " " \fICOLUMN\fR
Field to order by: \fIprincipal\fR (default), \fIrole\fR, \fIaccountId\fR
or \fIcreated\fR.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-sort\-direction " " \fIDIRECTION\fR
\fIasc\fR (default) or \fIdesc\fR.
.SH PAGING
The whole-account listing is the one that grows: it is one row per
principal per role, so an account with many users and several roles each
has more grants than is useful to read at once.
.PP
.I total
is how many grants match the filter, not how many this page holds \- so it
is what says whether there is another page, and it does not change as you
page through them.
.PP
Results are ordered by
.B \-\-sort\-column
whether or not you page. That is deliberate: paging an unordered
collection can show the same grant on two pages and never show another.
.SH OUTPUT
Each grant carries its
.IR grantId ,
the role, the principal, the account, the namespaces and resources it is
scoped to, and when and by whom it was granted.
.I total
counts every grant matching the filter, across all pages.
.SH WHO MAY RUN IT
Installation administrators \- members of the
.I administrator
user group. See
.B ADMINISTRATOR PRIVILEGES
in
.BR euclid-cli-eam (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The grants were listed. An empty list is a successful answer.
.TP
.B 1
Both
.B \-\-principal
and
.B \-\-role
were given, the request could not be sent, or the caller is not an
administrator. If the failure was an expired or invalid session
(\fIHTTP 401\fR), the stored credentials file is removed; run
.B euclid-cli eam login
again before retrying.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
# Everything granted in this account
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eam list-grants

# What one user holds in their own right
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eam list-grants \e
    --principal ern:eam:eu-central-1:000000000000:user:jdoe

# Who would be affected by narrowing a role
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eam list-grants \e
    --role order-service

# In another account
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eam list-grants \e
    --account 123456789012

# The first fifty, newest first
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eam list-grants \e
    --page-size 50 --sort-column created --sort-direction desc

# The second page of fifty
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eam list-grants \e
    --page-size 50 --page-index 1
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eam (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-grant-role (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-revoke-role (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-check-permission (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-list-roles (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-update-role (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-delete-role (1)
